Repair site iFixit today took apart Apple's <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/airpods/">AirPods 5</a> for one of its traditional teardown videos. It turns out the new AirPods are slightly more repairable than prior models, but it's still not a simple home fix.<br/>
<br/>
<div class="center-wrap"><iframe title="We've Given AirPods 0/10 for 10 Years. Until Now." src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/v7zBYbe0UZU" width="560" height="315" frameborder="0" allowfullscreen="allowfullscreen"></iframe></div><br/>
The &zwnj;AirPods 5&zwnj; use pronged batteries that Apple previously used in the AirPods 3, but not the AirPods 4. The battery can be removed with a "nifty pair of tongs," and iFixit says the component is "intuitive to unplug." When the AirPods 3 used the pronged battery, there was no easy way to get into the earbud, but the &zwnj;AirPods 5&zwnj; are easier to pry open.<br/>
<br/>
iFixit was able get into the &zwnj;AirPods 5&zwnj; with a hair dryer to heat the adhesive and a clamp to hold the earbud in place. The left and right earbuds use different batteries with reversed polarity, so the battery from a left AirPod can't be swapped into a right AirPod, and vice versa.<br/>
<br/>
While the earbuds have a better repair score, iFixit said the case is "still a disaster" and difficult to get into without destroying it.<br/>
<br/>
